OK, here is the current status:

I have a prototype architecture put together, but I have run into some
implementation difficulties with the current I/O system so I thought I
would throw out my idea to the group and get some feedback before doing

too much more.


I plan on implementing a string-based save system with 'marker' tags to

separate structures and fields.  The beginning of each struct will have

a tag that is the struct name and will end with a 0x01 string (1
character).  Each data element will be terminated with a 0xFF.  This
will allow the reading code to skip data it doesn't understand (added
at
the end) and quit early if there is more data than it can handle.  This

should suffice for minor changes in the number of items/monsters/etc.
because it can re-sync up with the data stream at each struct name and
scream bloody murder if it finds a struct name it doesn't recognize (or

just skip it.)


For demonstration purposes I will use Q and | so a file might look like

this:


verson_info|1234567890|1234567890|1234567890|1234567890|Q|bonesid|bonD0.7|Q=
=AD|fruit|...



The primary difficulty I have encountered is all of the current code
assumes (because of the version_info) that it knows _exactly_ how much
data to read in and that assumption is required for the compression to
work.  Under my scheme, you don't know how much you need to read in
until you have read it.


If this scheme seems reasonable, I propose ignoring all of the current
file code for now and just implementing my own.  One downside is I'll
skip the compression for now to avoid compicating the initial
implementation and to ease debugging.


Any feedback?


